-- ═══ 52 RETIRE ═══
UPDATE scholarships SET is_active = false, application_status = 'closed', award_amount = NULL, award_currency = NULL, award_period = NULL, award_basis = NULL, living_allowance = NULL, funding_type = NULL, tuition_coverage = NULL, english_requirements = NULL, required_documents = NULL, expected_opening_period = NULL, expected_deadline_period = NULL, rounds = NULL, notes = $note$Retired on 2026-10-27 (cleanup pass 109) after official-source review: there is no single Université PSL award named 'PSL Excellence Scholarship'. The previously stored URL (psl.eu/en/education/scholarships) returns 404, and PSL's current official 'Scholarships and grants for graduate students' page (fetched 2026-10-27) presents the master's excellence landscape as a portfolio of SEPARATE graduate-programme grants - Excellence Grants in Chemistry, in Engineering (ISAI, international students), in Computer Science, Mathematics, Finance, Earth and Biodiversity Sciences, Life Sciences (IMaGHE curriculum only), Religious Studies, Management (M2 only) and Physics (ICFP, international students) - each funded by its own PSL Graduate Program with its own programme scope, value and application route (typical published values include €1,200/month for 10 months in Computer Science and Mathematics and €12,000/year in Finance), and the page expressly refers applicants to each graduate program's own webpage for details. A singular umbrella title over that programme-level portfolio is a directory entry, not one scholarship an applicant applies for, and picking a single programme's grant to rewrite this row into would violate the cleanup's identity rule, so the record is retired. The unified PSL funding instrument on the same page - the PhD Track Grants - is catalogued separately as record 128. Individual graduate-programme excellence grants (e.g. Computer Science, Mathematics, Finance) are candidates for separately verified records in a future additions batch. Kept for audit only; not a discoverable scholarship. university_scholarships row 468 withdrawn; source 2597 repointed to PSL's current official scholarships-and-grants overview page.$note$, last_updated_at = now() WHERE id = 52;

-- ═══ 128 PSL PhD Track Grants ═══
UPDATE scholarships SET title = 'PSL PhD Track Grants', provider = 'Université PSL', coverage_type = 'Full Funding', amount_usd_value = 0, deadline = 'September 2027 intake: applications via the PSL portal from 14 October to 14 December 2026 (11.59pm CET); interviews January-March 2027, results February-April 2027 depending on the track. The grant is awarded upon admission - there is no separate scholarship application.', deadline_date = '2026-12-14', deadline_type = 'fixed', deadline_range_start = NULL, deadline_range_end = NULL, degree_levels = $j$["Master's", "PhD"]$j$, eligible_majors = $j$["PhD tracks open for September 2027: Chemistry, Life Sciences, Astrophysics, Computer Science (the Physics, Engineering, Earth Sciences & Biodiversity and Cognitive Sciences tracks will not reopen for 2027)"]$j$, eligible_countries = '[]', min_gpa = NULL, min_ielts = NULL, financial_need_based = false, merit_based = true, description = $desc$The PSL PhD Track Grants fund Université PSL's PhD tracks - highly selective, entirely English-taught five-year programmes (two years of Master's followed by three years of PhD) for high-potential students aiming at top-level research careers, with every admitted student supported for the full duration. Phase 1 (Master's): a scholarship of €10,000 per year for two years, disbursed as €1,000 a month over ten months per year, awarded upon admission with no separate application required. Phase 2 (PhD): a three-year doctoral contract paying a minimum gross salary of €2,300 per month under French legislation, with employee status and benefits. The funding does not cover tuition, housing or transport costs - published tuition runs at the low national rates (€254/year at Master's level and €397/year at doctorate level for most tracks, plus the CVEC of about €105) - but first-year housing in partner residences is guaranteed (rent payable by the student) and a welcome pack includes the PSL Welcome Desk, free French classes and university healthcare. Admission is only into Master's Year 1 for the full five years (no direct M2 or PhD entry), via a document-based application on the PSL portal by 14 December 2026 for the September 2027 intake followed by remote interviews in English; for 2027 four tracks recruit (Chemistry, Life Sciences, Astrophysics and Computer Science), while Physics, Engineering, Earth Sciences & Biodiversity and Cognitive Sciences do not reopen. Selection weighs academic excellence, the quality of the research project, and (highly recommended) previous laboratory experience; qualifying for the PhD phase requires approval of the dissertation project through a qualifying examination after the Master's.$desc$, requirements = $req$Open to all nationalities. Applicants must hold at least a Bachelor's degree (or equivalent, 180 ECTS) or be in its final year (holders of a Master's are also eligible but still enter at Master's Year 1); meet the entry requirements of the Master's degree concerned; show academic excellence and a quality research project, with previous laboratory experience highly recommended; and prove a very good command of English - level C1, compulsory proof (e.g. TOEFL/IELTS) for non-native speakers unless the previous degree was earned at an English-speaking university (older results may be submitted provisionally). The application (all documents in English) comprises an academic CV, transcripts and diplomas, a strict one-page statement of purpose, a one-to-two-page research project, the English proof, and contact details of at least two academic referees. Admission to the five-year track and the regular two-year Master's are distinct processes and candidates are advised to apply to both. C1 English is a qualitative level on this page (test-specific numbers sit on the application portal), so min_ielts is not recorded, and no numeric GPA cutoff is published, so min_gpa is also NULL.$req$, website_url = 'https://psl.eu/en/education/find-your-curriculum/psl-phd-tracks', source_url = 'https://psl.eu/en/education/find-your-curriculum/psl-phd-tracks', application_url = 'https://espacecandidature.psl.eu/s/formationsPSL?language=en_US', recurrence = 'annual', application_status = 'open', verification_status = 'verified', source_reliability = 10, is_active = true, last_verified_at = now(), last_updated_at = now(), notes = $note$REWRITTEN 2026-10-27 (cleanup pass 109) from Université PSL's official PSL PhD Tracks page and the 'Scholarships and grants for graduate students' overview, both fetched the same day (5-year fully funded English-taught Master's+PhD; Phase 1 scholarship €10,000/year for 2 years disbursed €1,000 x 10 months and awarded upon admission with no separate application; Phase 2 a 3-year doctoral contract at a €2,300/month minimum gross salary per the 29 August 2016 decree; funding excludes tuition/housing/transport, with low national tuition rates (€254/€397 + ~€105 CVEC per the FAQ) and guaranteed first-year housing at the student's rent; September 2027 admissions calendar - applications 14 October to 14 December 2026 23.59 CET exclusively via the PSL portal, remote English interviews January-March 2027, results February-April 2027; recruiting tracks for 2027 are Chemistry, Life Sciences, Astrophysics and Computer Science, with Physics, Engineering, Earth Sciences & Biodiversity and Cognitive Sciences not reopening; entry only at Master's Year 1; Bachelor's/180 ECTS minimum, C1 English proof, academic-excellence + research-project + laboratory-experience criteria; application package as listed). Award is NULL with basis 'variable' (EUR, 'year'): funding has two structurally different phases (a €10,000/year master's grant, then a €2,300/month minimum-gross doctoral salary), so both figures live in funding_type; individual tracks may pitch the same envelope differently (the Computer Science track page advertises €12,000/year, €1,200 x 10 months), which the variable encoding covers. degree_levels corrected to Master's+PhD (entry is post-Bachelor into M1; the previous record said PhD only). Title normalised to the scheme's official name from the PSL overview ('Université PSL PhD Tracks grants'); the previously stored URL returns 404. deadline_date 2026-12-14 is the live 2027-intake closing with status 'open'. min_gpa/min_ielts NULL. university_scholarships row 469 synced; source 262 repointed from the bare domain to the fetched official PhD Tracks page. Previous record held the scheme at reliability 4 with no values read.$note$, award_amount = NULL, award_currency = 'EUR', award_period = 'year', award_basis = 'variable', living_allowance = NULL, funding_type = 'Phase 1 (Master''s): €10,000/year scholarship for 2 years (€1,000 x 10 months; awarded at admission); Phase 2 (PhD): 3-year doctoral contract at a €2,300/month minimum gross salary. Tuition, housing and transport are not covered; first-year housing is guaranteed (rent payable).', tuition_coverage = 'Tuition is not waived, but rates are the low French national ones (€254/year at Master''s level and €397/year at doctorate level for most tracks, all nationalities, plus about €105 CVEC) - see the PhD Track FAQ for the current decree figures.', english_requirements = 'Very good command of English (level C1 recommended); official proof (e.g. TOEFL/IELTS) is compulsory for non-native speakers unless the previous degree was earned at an English-speaking university.', required_documents = $j$["Academic CV; transcripts and diplomas from each institution attended (single merged PDF)", "Statement of purpose in English (strictly 1 page)", "Research project/statement in English (1-2 pages)", "Proof of English proficiency at C1 level (e.g. TOEFL/IELTS) or a statement of English-medium prior study", "Contact details of at least two academic referees"]$j$, expected_opening_period = NULL, expected_deadline_period = NULL, rounds = $json$[{"intake": "September 2027", "opens": "2026-10-14", "deadline": "2026-12-14", "interviews": "2027-01 to 2027-03", "results": "2027-02 to 2027-04", "status": "open"}, {"pattern": "Annual: applications open mid-October and close mid-December for the following September intake; grants are awarded automatically upon admission"}]$json$ WHERE id = 128;

-- ═══ 151 IP Paris PhD Track Excellence Scholarships ═══
UPDATE scholarships SET title = 'IP Paris PhD Track Excellence Scholarships', provider = 'Institut Polytechnique de Paris (IP Paris)', coverage_type = 'Full Funding', amount_usd_value = 0, deadline = 'For the 2027-2028 academic year, PhD Track applications run from 28 October 2026 to 14 January 2027 via the IP Paris online application platform. Scholarship assessment is automatic: admitted files are reviewed by a jury and IP Paris contacts those awarded funding (others are placed on a funding waiting list) - there is no separate scholarship application.', deadline_date = '2027-01-14', deadline_type = 'fixed', deadline_range_start = NULL, deadline_range_end = NULL, degree_levels = $j$["Master's", "PhD"]$j$, eligible_majors = $j$["The 12 PhD Tracks: Advanced Materials; Bioengineering and Quantitative Life Sciences; Chemistry and Interfaces; Computer Science; Data & Artificial Intelligence; Economics; Electrical Engineering; Energy for Climate; Mathematics; Mechanics; Physics; Quantum Science and Technologies (QUANTIX)"]$j$, eligible_countries = '[]', min_gpa = NULL, min_ielts = NULL, financial_need_based = false, merit_based = true, description = $desc$The IP Paris PhD Track Excellence Scholarships fund the Institut Polytechnique de Paris PhD Track - a five-year, English-taught research programme of excellence (two Master's years plus three PhD years, 420 ECTS) for high-potential Bachelor's holders who want an international research career, with students immersed in IP Paris laboratories and individually supervised from the first year. Each year IP Paris awards excellence scholarships to PhD Track students in three types: the Full Package (FP) funds five (or four) years end-to-end - a Master's excellence scholarship of €10,000 per year for two (or one) years plus a three-year doctoral funding commitment with a monthly salary (minimum gross funding of about €82,800 over the three doctoral years); the Master's Scholarship (BM) is the €10,000-per-year Master's excellence scholarship alone for two (or one) years; and the Doctoral Package (DP) is the three-year doctoral funding commitment alone. Independent of scholarships, tuition is subsidised for every admitted PhD Track student - reduced from about €7,000 to €243 per year in Years 1-2 and set at €380 per year in Years 3-5 (official ministry rates for 2023-2024, subject to decree). There is no separate scholarship application and the admissions team does not evaluate scholarship requests: admitted candidates' files are automatically reviewed by a jury, IP Paris contacts those awarded funding, and the rest are placed on a funding waiting list. The programme recruits across twelve tracks (from Advanced Materials and Computer Science to Economics and Quantum Science), classes begin in September, and candidates hear back within about two months of the session's end.$desc$, requirements = $req$Entry to the PhD Track requires a Bachelor's degree (Bac+3, 180 ECTS or international equivalent) for M1 entry; direct entry into the second year may be offered to holders of a Master's degree or 240 ECTS of post-secondary education (to be requested in the statement of purpose). Applicants must be fluent in English, have an excellent academic background and wish to become researchers; up to five programmes (Master's and/or PhD Track) may be applied to, each with its own specially written résumé and supporting statement, and applicants must certify their ECTS credits (non-ECTS systems need an official grading-equivalence document; GPAs are converted to the French 20-point scale in the application). Funding eligibility is decided by a jury after admission - there is no separate scholarship form and no published GPA or IELTS cutoff for the funding itself, so min_gpa/min_ielts are not recorded. The scholarships and the tuition subsidy are specific to PhD Track students; the standalone Master's and three-year PhD routes at IP Paris have their own admissions and funding pages.$req$, website_url = 'https://www.ip-paris.fr/en/education/graduate-programs/phd-track', source_url = 'https://www.ip-paris.fr/en/education/useful-information/scholarships', application_url = 'https://application-portal.ip-paris.fr/', recurrence = 'annual', application_status = 'upcoming', verification_status = 'verified', source_reliability = 10, is_active = true, last_verified_at = now(), last_updated_at = now(), notes = $note$REWRITTEN 2026-10-27 (cleanup pass 109) from IP Paris's official PhD Track page, Scholarships page and PhD Track FAQ, all fetched the same day (five-year English-taught Master's+PhD of excellence, 420 ECTS; three scholarship types for PhD Track students - Full Package: €10,000/year Master's excellence scholarship for 2 (or 1) years plus a 3-year doctoral funding commitment of about €82,800 minimum gross total; Master's Scholarship: the €10,000/year Master's award alone; Doctoral Package: the doctoral commitment alone; tuition subsidised for all admitted PhD Track students to €243/year in Years 1-2 and €380/year in Years 3-5 at 2023-2024 ministry rates; funding decided by a jury automatically after admission with a funding waiting list - no separate application; Bachelor's 180 ECTS for M1, possible Year-2 entry at 240 ECTS/Master's; fluent English; 12 named tracks; up to five programme applications; application window for 2027-2028 of 28 October 2026 to 14 January 2027). Award is NULL with basis 'variable' (EUR, 'year'): the three packages and two programme phases carry structurally different values, so the figures live in funding_type (the €82,800 three-year gross floor corresponds to the €2,300/month national doctoral-contract minimum, noted as approximate by IP Paris). deadline_date 2027-01-14 with status 'upcoming' because the window opens on 28 October 2026, the day after this verification. This record covers only the PhD Track funding; record 604 covers the IP Paris Master's excellence scholarships for regular Master's students. university_id set to the existing Institut Polytechnique de Paris record (302, previously NULL) and a university_scholarships row added; source 2544 repointed from the bare domain to the official IP Paris Scholarships page (the funding-terms authority), with website_url at the PhD Track page. min_gpa/min_ielts NULL. Previous record held the scheme at reliability 4 with a 404 URL.$note$, award_amount = NULL, award_currency = 'EUR', award_period = 'year', award_basis = 'variable', living_allowance = NULL, funding_type = 'Three scholarship types: Full Package (€10,000/year Master''s excellence scholarship for 2 years + 3-year doctoral funding commitment, approx. €82,800 minimum gross over 3 years); Master''s Scholarship (€10,000/year); Doctoral Package (3-year doctoral funding commitment). Tuition subsidised to €243/year (Years 1-2) and €380/year (Years 3-5) for all admitted PhD Track students.', tuition_coverage = 'Tuition fees are subsidised for all PhD Track admitted students, scholarship or not: about €7,000/year brought down to €243/year in Years 1-2 and €380/year in Years 3-5 (ministry rates for 2023-2024, subject to decree).', english_requirements = 'Applicants must be fluent in English (the programme is taught in English); no scholarship-specific IELTS figure is published.', required_documents = $j$["PhD Track application via the IP Paris online application platform (résumé and supporting statement written specifically for the programme)", "Document certifying ECTS credits earned (and an official grading-system explanation for non-ECTS systems)", "Academic transcripts with GPA/average converted to the 20-point scale and institutions attended over the past three years"]$j$, expected_opening_period = NULL, expected_deadline_period = NULL, rounds = $json$[{"intake": "2027-2028", "opens": "2026-10-28", "deadline": "2027-01-14", "status": "upcoming"}, {"pattern": "Annual: application period runs from October to January; scholarship juries review admitted files automatically and answer within about two months of the session end"}]$json$, university_id = 302 WHERE id = 151;

-- ═══ 344 Doctoral Contracts at Sorbonne ═══
UPDATE scholarships SET title = 'Doctoral Contracts (Contrats Doctoraux) at Sorbonne Université', provider = 'Sorbonne Université', coverage_type = 'Doctoral Employment Contract (salary)', amount_usd_value = 0, deadline = 'Each Sorbonne doctoral school runs an annual recruitment campaign (typically in spring) awarding doctoral contracts for thesis projects - procedures, project lists and dates are published on each doctoral school''s website and via ADUM; laboratory-funded projects can additionally open throughout the year. First secure a thesis supervisor/topic, then compete in the relevant school campaign.', deadline_date = NULL, deadline_type = 'not_published', deadline_range_start = NULL, deadline_range_end = NULL, degree_levels = $j$["PhD"]$j$, eligible_majors = $j$["All"]$j$, eligible_countries = '[]', min_gpa = NULL, min_ielts = NULL, financial_need_based = false, merit_based = true, description = $desc$The doctoral contract (contrat doctoral) is the standard funded-doctorate status at Sorbonne Université - a three-year fixed-term public-law employment contract with a nationally fixed minimum salary, currently €2,300 gross per month since 1 January 2026 for a 100%-research contract (the scale was revalued annually: €2,044 in 2023, €2,100 in 2024, €2,200 in 2025, €2,300 from 2026), with the option of devoting one sixth of working time to a complementary mission such as teaching. Sorbonne's doctoral schools each organise an annual recruitment campaign in which they award the university's doctoral contracts competitively to candidates carrying out an approved doctoral research project: topics are published by laboratories and supervisors, candidates contact the proposing supervisor and apply (in many schools through the ADUM portal), and a school-level competition ranks them. The same contract form also carries the university's other funded routes - the Doctoral College's interdisciplinary programme calls (e.g. Interfaces for Living Beings, SCAI artificial intelligence), disability doctoral contracts with the ministry, laboratory-raised funding (national/European/company contracts) and CIFRE industrial doctorates - because French public institutions recruit all funded doctoral candidates under this single status whatever the money's origin. Contracted doctoral candidates are employees with health coverage, leave and other non-tenured civil-servant provisions, a two-month probationary period, and compulsory annual re-registration. There is no age or diploma-recency limit, and foreign-national candidates admitted to register for a thesis by a doctoral school may apply on the same footing; holders of foreign-government scholarships below a school's required minimum can sometimes be topped up by their research unit (ASDB aid).$desc$, requirements = $req$In practice a candidate needs a Master's degree (or equivalent giving access to doctoral studies), a thesis project with a Sorbonne Université supervisor attached to one of its doctoral schools, and admission by that school; the contract itself is then won either in the school's annual competitive campaign (project-based - usually one candidate per published project, selected on the file and often an audition/interview) or attached to a laboratory's own funded project. By decree there is no age limit and no time limit since the diploma, and students of foreign nationality admitted to register for a thesis may apply for a doctoral contract. Campaign calendars, eligible topics and file requirements are set by each doctoral school in its internal rules and on its website (Sorbonne coordinates some campaigns centrally, e.g. the LLSHS humanities competition). The €2,300 gross monthly figure recorded here is the national minimum for a research-only contract in force from 1 January 2026 - a floor, not a cap; complementary missions (up to one sixth of working time) are remunerated additionally. No GPA or IELTS cutoff is published for the contract itself, so min_gpa/min_ielts are not recorded; language expectations follow the host laboratory and the thesis project.$req$, website_url = 'https://www.sorbonne-universite.fr/en/doctorate/application-and-admission/funding', source_url = 'https://www.sorbonne-universite.fr/en/doctorate/application-and-admission/funding', application_url = 'https://www.sorbonne-universite.fr/en/doctorate/application-and-admission/funding', recurrence = 'annual', application_status = 'upcoming', verification_status = 'verified', source_reliability = 9, is_active = true, last_verified_at = now(), last_updated_at = now(), notes = $note$REWRITTEN 2026-10-27 (cleanup pass 109) from Sorbonne Université's official doctorate Funding page (fetched the same day; page updated 24/07/2025) corroborated by the university's doctoral-school pages found in the same audit (the doctoral contract as the reference legal framework: 3-year fixed-term employment contract, 2-month probation, national minimum salary, complementary missions up to 1/6 of working time, decree of 29 August 2016; each doctoral school organises an annual recruitment campaign awarding university doctoral contracts, with procedures in the schools' internal rules and websites; Doctoral College interdisciplinary calls and laboratory/CIFRE funding concluding the same contract; ASDB top-ups for under-funded scholarship holders; and, from the ED393/ED515/ED406 school pages, the salary revaluation path €2,044 gross (Jan 2023) -> €2,100 (2024) -> €2,200 (2025) -> €2,300 (from 1 January 2026), one-candidate-per-project competitive procedure via ADUM, and explicit eligibility of foreign-national thesis registrants with no age or diploma-recency limit). Reliability 9 (not 10): the contract is France's employment-status funding category rather than a single named call, so campaign dates and topic lists are necessarily school-level; the €2,300 figure is the verifiable national gross minimum for a 100%-research contract and is stored as a fixed monthly award on that floor basis. This mirrors the catalogue's salary-based doctoral records (e.g. UZH Candoc). deadline_date NULL/'not_published' with the annual spring campaign pattern in rounds; status 'upcoming' relative to the main campaigns. Title clarified from the stored singular ('Sorbonne University Doctoral School Contract'); the previously stored URL returns 404. min_gpa/min_ielts NULL. university_scholarships row 586 synced; source 2619 repointed from the bare domain to the fetched official funding page. Previous record held the scheme at reliability 4 with no values read.$note$, award_amount = 2300, award_currency = 'EUR', award_period = 'month', award_basis = 'fixed', living_allowance = NULL, funding_type = '3-year doctoral employment contract at the national minimum salary (€2,300 gross/month since 1 January 2026 for a research-only contract; complementary teaching/expertise missions up to 1/6 of time paid additionally)', tuition_coverage = 'The contract is a salary; university enrolment fees follow standard French doctoral registration (not a tuition waiver scheme).', english_requirements = 'No IELTS cutoff is set for the contract; working language depends on the laboratory and thesis project (many Sorbonne laboratories work in English).', required_documents = $j$["Thesis project with a Sorbonne Université supervisor (candidates contact the supervisor proposing a published project; typically one candidate per project)", "Application file in the doctoral school's annual competition (often a single PDF via the ADUM portal, per the school's internal rules)", "Master's degree (or equivalent) and academic record supporting thesis admission by the doctoral school"]$j$, expected_opening_period = NULL, expected_deadline_period = NULL, rounds = $json$[{"pattern": "Annual: each doctoral school runs a spring recruitment campaign for its doctoral contracts (topics published by laboratories; applications e.g. via ADUM in late spring); lab-funded and CIFRE contracts may open year-round"}]$json$ WHERE id = 344;

